Given a parabola $C$: $y^{2}=2px (p > 0)$ with focus $F$, and a point $(3,m)$ on the parabola $C$ with a distance of $5$ to the focus.

$(1)$ Find the equation of the parabola $C$;

$(2)$ Draw a line $l$ through point $F$ intersecting the parabola $C$ at points $A$ and $B$. If the $y$-coordinate of the midpoint of line segment $AB$ is $-1$, find the equation of line $l$.

response:
The system of equations is:
\[
\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
x + ky + 3z &= 0 \quad \text{(1)} \\
x + ky - 2z &= 0 \quad (2) \\
2x + 4y - 3z &= 0 \quad (3)
\endalign*}
\]
Since the system has a non-zero solution, the determinant of the coefficient matrix is zero:
\[
\boxed{
\begin{vmatrix}
1 & k & 3 \\
1 & k & -2 \\
2 & 4 & -3
\end{bmatrix} = 1 \left( \pm 1 \right)
\]
The determinant is:
\[
\boxed{
\boxed{
\boxed{
\begin{vmatrix}
1 & k & 3 \\
1 & k & -2 \\
2 & 4 & -3
\end{vmatrix} = 1 \left( k(-3) - 4(-2) \right) - k \left( 1(-3) - 2(-2) \right) + 3 \left( 1 \cdot 4 - 2 \cdot k \right) = 1(-3k + 8) - k(-3 + 4) + 3(4 - 2k) = -3k + 8 - k + 12 - 6k = -10k + 20 = 0.
}}
\]
Thus, $-10k + 20 = 0$, which gives $k = 2$. Substituting \( k = 2 \), the system becomes:
\[
\boxed{
\boxed{
\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
